tests/ui/check/err_cost_refuted.sky:2:5: note: cost of `mm`: 2·K·M·N flops, 1 allocation of M·N floats
  |
2 | def mm(A: Matrix[N, K], B: Matrix[K, M]) -> Matrix[N, M]:
  |     ^^
  = note: memory traffic: 8·K·M + 8·K·N bytes read, 8·M·N bytes written

tests/ui/check/err_cost_refuted.sky:3:5: error: `mm` costs 2·K·M·N flops, which is more than `2 * N^3`
  |
3 |     cost flops <= 2 * N^3
  |     ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ the cost is more than this
4 |     return A @ B
  |            ----- `A @ B` costs 2·K·M·N flops
  = note: for example with K = 2, M = 1 and N = 1: it costs 4 flops, and the contract says 2
  = note: the dominant term of the excess is 2·K·M·N, which the bound does not cover
  = note: proven false by the static cost model: a cost contract holds for every size from 1 up, lower-order terms included
  = help: the cost is exactly 2·K·M·N: write `cost flops <= 2 * K * M * N`

tests/ui/check/err_cost_refuted.sky:6:5: note: cost of `step`: 2·N flops, 1 allocation of N floats
  |
6 | def step(v: Vector[N], w: Vector[N]) -> Vector[N]:
  |     ^^^^
  = note: memory traffic: 16·N bytes read, 8·N bytes written

tests/ui/check/err_cost_refuted.sky:7:5: error: `step` costs 1 allocation, not 0 as `cost allocations == 0` states
  |
7 |     cost allocations == 0
  |     ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ the cost is more than this
8 |     return v + 2 * w
  |            --------- `v + 2 * w` costs 1 allocation
  = note: proven false by the static cost model: a cost contract holds for every size from 1 up, lower-order terms included

tests/ui/check/err_cost_refuted.sky:8:12: note: [FUSE-001] `v + 2 * w` is rewritten to `fused(2, w, v)`
  |
8 |     return v + 2 * w
  |            ^^^^^^^^^
  = note: applied by the `fuse` pass
  = note: because fused 2 elementwise operations into one kernel; saves 1 temporary array of shape Vector[N]
  = note: because every intermediate result is used exactly once, by the next operation, and every array operand is a Vector[N]
  = note: because 2 of the operations can fail (`mul`, `add`); the kernel reports the failure of the first of them in program order that fails at any element, which is the error the separate operations gave

tests/ui/check/err_cost_refuted.sky:8:12: note: [PAR-001] this elementwise kernel runs on 8 threads once it has at least 2048 elements
  |
8 |     return v + 2 * w
  |            ^^^^^^^^^
  = note: because its size is known only when the program runs; below the threshold splitting costs more than it saves
  = note: because every element is independent, so the result is bit-identical to the sequential one whatever the split
  = note: because 2 of its operations can fail (`mul`, `add`): if chunks fail, the failing operation that comes first in program order is reported, at its lowest failing element, which is the error a sequential run gives

tests/ui/check/err_cost_refuted.sky:10:5: note: cost of `lower_order`: 2·N³ + N² flops, 2 allocations of N² floats
   |
10 | def lower_order(A: Matrix[N, N]) -> Matrix[N, N]:
   |     ^^^^^^^^^^^
12 |     return A @ A + A
   |            ----- hot spot: `A @ A` costs 2·N³ flops
   |            --------- hot spot: `A @ A + A` costs N² flops
   = note: memory traffic: 32·N² bytes read, 16·N² bytes written

tests/ui/check/err_cost_refuted.sky:11:5: error: `lower_order` costs 2·N³ + N² flops, which is more than `2 * N^2`
   |
11 |     cost flops <= 2 * N^2
   |     ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ the cost is more than this
12 |     return A @ A + A
   |            ----- `A @ A` costs 2·N³ flops
   = note: for example with N = 1: it costs 3 flops, and the contract says 2
   = note: the dominant term of the excess is 2·N³, which the bound does not cover
   = note: proven false by the static cost model: a cost contract holds for every size from 1 up, lower-order terms included
   = help: the cost is exactly 2·N³ + N²: write `cost flops <= 2 * N^3 + N^2`

tests/ui/check/err_cost_refuted.sky:12:12: note: [PAR-001] this elementwise `add` runs on 8 threads once it has at least 262144 elements
   |
12 |     return A @ A + A
   |            ^^^^^^^^^
   = note: because its size is known only when the program runs; below the threshold splitting costs more than it saves
   = note: because every element is independent, so the result is bit-identical to the sequential one whatever the split
   = note: because its only failure is an overflow (or a zero divisor) of the whole operation, found after the elements are computed and reported the same way whichever thread found it

tests/ui/check/err_cost_refuted.sky:14:5: note: cost of `loop`: N² flops, N allocations (N² floats in all)
   |
14 | def loop(v: Vector[N]) -> Vector[N]:
   |     ^^^^
   = note: memory traffic: 8·N² bytes read, 8·N² bytes written

tests/ui/check/err_cost_refuted.sky:15:5: error: `loop` costs N² flops, which is more than `1000 * N`
   |
15 |     cost flops <= 1000 * N
   |     ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ the cost is more than this
17 |         v = v * 2
   |             ----- `v * 2` costs N² flops
   = note: for example with N = 10000: it costs 100000000 flops, and the contract says 10000000
   = note: the dominant term of the excess is N², which the bound does not cover
   = note: proven false by the static cost model: a cost contract holds for every size from 1 up, lower-order terms included
   = help: the cost is exactly N²: write `cost flops <= N^2`

